From 12856aa052ed872ae3170b162444f1f8f6672092 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: terminills <tdeters@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 19:52:51 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 622/638] aros: link libpthread after the objects so one
 archive supplies every pthread_*

chrome was carrying two libpthread copies. The toolchain lists the sysroot
libpthread.a in extra_ldflags with -u pthread_mutex_unlock; the archive comes
before the program's objects, so ld extracts only that member. collect-aros then
satisfies the remaining pthread_* from the libpthread.a compiled into the
crosstools as OBJLIBDIR - the hosted toolchain-build copy, which ignores
--sysroot and dated from 09-06. That copy predates 70d3c739 (pthread_attr_t
gained cpuaffinity), so its StarterFunc read ThreadInfo.tlsvalues at 0xe0 while
the sysroot pthread_setspecific wrote at 0xe8: every key destructor at thread
exit received the previous key's value. Chromium's key-4 destructor got emutls'
untagged pointer, BoringSSL's key-5 destructor got Chromium's tagged one and
took the Chrome_ChildIOThread #GP in thread_local_destructor.

Adding pthread to base's libs puts -lpthread after the objects, so every
pthread_* resolves from the sysroot archive and collect-aros never re-supplies.
Verified: pthread_create 0x3c5 / StarterFunc 0x48e (sysroot sizes); HeavyBase
renders with all exit destructors receiving their own key's value.

Side effect worth knowing: the old pthread_create ignored the affinity attr,
so --aros-pin-workers had never actually placed a thread off CPU 0 until now.

---
 base/BUILD.gn | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/base/BUILD.gn b/base/BUILD.gn
index 7a3f228e2e..c65396828a 100644
--- a/base/BUILD.gn
+++ b/base/BUILD.gn
@@ -1161,9 +1161,19 @@ component("base") {
   if (is_aros) {
     # debug.library for LoadSeg symbol lookup; liblinuxso (static, from the
     # AROS SDK) for Linux ET_DYN objects such as the Widevine CDM.
+    #
+    # libpthread must be a lib (placed after the program's objects), not only
+    # the archive the toolchain names in extra_ldflags: that one precedes the
+    # objects, so ld extracts nothing from it beyond the -u member, and every
+    # other pthread_* symbol is left for collect-aros to satisfy from the
+    # libpthread.a compiled into the crosstools - a stale copy from a different
+    # build with a different ThreadInfo layout. The result was one binary with
+    # pthread_setspecific from the sysroot and StarterFunc from the old archive:
+    # every key destructor at thread exit received the previous key's value.
     libs += [
       "debug",
       "linuxso",
+      "pthread",
     ]
   }
 
